 ## Background
-Spatial resampling is a fundamental operation in volume rendering, originating from the need to align volumetric data with different coordinate systems or to increase resolution for better visual fidelity. Early implementations relied on nearest-neighbor or trilinear interpolation due to computational constraints. The development of level-set grids in libraries like OpenVDB introduced specialized resampling tools that preserve topological properties [S2, unofficial]. In the context of view synthesis, double-resolution preprocessing was introduced to mitigate discrete positioning errors in multi-view volumetric compression [S1]. Modern pipelines often combine these deterministic resampling techniques with deep learning-based super-resolution to balance computational efficiency and perceptual quality.
+Spatial resampling is a fundamental operation in volume rendering, originating from the need to align volumetric data with different coordinate systems or to increase resolution for better visual fidelity [G]. Early implementations relied on nearest-neighbor or trilinear interpolation due to computational constraints [G]. The development of level-set grids in libraries like OpenVDB introduced specialized resampling tools that rebuild the narrow band to match reference transforms [S2, unofficial]. In the context of view synthesis, double-resolution preprocessing was introduced to mitigate discrete positioning errors in multi-view volumetric compression [S1]. Modern pipelines often combine these deterministic resampling techniques with deep learning-based super-resolution to balance computational efficiency and perceptual quality [G].
 
